|> Exercise
    handle=_lorentz_factor_dimensionality_

    if $v$ and $c$ are both speeds, what
    is the dimension of...
    !! this expression:
    $$
    \sqrt{1 - {v^2\over c^2}}
    $$
    ...?

    |> Solution

        Since 
        $$
        {v\over c}
        $$
        
        is “speed over speed” is dimensionless,
        and since the sums, differences, squares,
        and square roots, etc, of dimensionless
        quantities is dimensionless, the entire
        expression is dimensionless.

        |> Pause

        _Vocabulary._
        When $c$ is the speed of light
        the reciprocal of this expression, often
        written ‘$\gamma$’, is known as the
        _Lorentz factor_ in physics. It is also dimensionless:

        $$
        \gamma = {1\over \sqrt{1 - {v^2\over c^2}}}.
        $$

        !! (Also dimensionless: “1 over dimensionless”
        !! is dimensionless.)
        !! (Because the reciprocal of a dimensionless
        !! quantity is dimensionless, the Lorentz factor
        !! is also dimensionless.)

        !! ...known as the _Lorentz factor_ in physics.
        !! presuming $c$ is the speed of light.
        !! Because the reciprocal of a dimensionless
        !! quantity is dimensionless, the Lorentz factor
        !! is just as dimensionless as its reciprocal,
        !! i.e., the square root that we studied.

        !! |> SolutionNote

        !!     Because the reciprocal of a dimensionless
        !!     quantity is dimensionless, the Lorentz factor
        !!     is also dimensionless. (In case you had any nagging
        !!     doubts.)

|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensionality_t_over_v_

    If $x$ is a spatial displacement and $v$ is speed, what is
    the dimension of $x/v$?

    |> Solution

        It is time, since speed has the form
        “distance over time”, which cancels the “distance”
        dimension on top:

        $$
        {\te{DISTANCE}\over\te{SPEED}} = {\te{DISTANCE}\over \left({\te{DISTANCE} \over {\te{TIME}}}\right)} = {\te{DISTANCE}}\cdot {\te{TIME}\over \te{DISTANCE}} = \te{TIME}.
        $$

        |> SolutionNote

            What you see above are cancellations of
            dimensions (or units, in a related computation),
            not exact cancellation of quantities—what interests
            us is the _dimension_ of the final quantity,
            not its exact value.

        |> SolutionNote

            It is a good rule of thumb to remember that
            “one over speed” converts distance to time via
            multiplication, just like speed converts time
            to distance via multiplication. One is a consequence of the other: